**Running title.** BMD raises knee OA yet protects against fracture

## Highlights
- Higher genetically predicted BMD raises knee OA but protects against fracture.
- Across 13 skeletal phenotypes, BMD, OA and fracture form three genetic blocks.
- BMD→OA effect is only 9.5–33.2% explained by skeletal size (height proxy).
- Reverse OA→BMD and OA→fracture estimates were non-significant, as predicted.
- A systemic bone-forming set-point explains the opposing endpoint consequences.

**Keywords.** osteoarthritis; bone mineral density; fracture; linkage disequilibrium score regression; Mendelian randomisation; genetic correlation

---

## Abstract

Whether higher bone mineral density (BMD) reflects shared aetiology with or causation of osteoarthritis (OA) is unsettled, although people with OA tend to have higher BMD. We quantified genetic sharing and causal direction across 13 skeletal phenotypes (two OA definitions, ten regional BMD sites, any-fracture) using linkage-disequilibrium score regression across 78 phenotype pairs and two-sample Mendelian randomisation (IVW, MR-Egger, weighted median, MR-PRESSO, leave-one-out), with BMD's protection against fracture as a positive control. BMD sites inter-correlated strongly (r_g 0.69–0.96) and negatively with fracture (r_g −0.45 to −0.60); both OA definitions correlated positively with every BMD site (r_g 0.12–0.25) and with each other (r_g 0.84). Mendelian randomisation reproduced the positive control (OR 1.40 per 1 SD lower BMD, p=3.7×10⁻⁵¹) and showed genetically higher BMD raising knee-OA risk (β +0.017 to +0.028 per SD; p_IVW ≤6.4×10⁻⁴). Heterogeneity was high (I² 68–86%) but matched the positive control (78–83%), and all five estimates kept a positive direction across IVW, MR-Egger (intercepts p>0.24), weighted median and MR-PRESSO; 2 of 5 remained significant under random effects (3 of 5 under DerSimonian–Laird). Conditioning on height removed 9.5–33.2% of the effect, leaving 67–91% independent of the height–size pathway. Reverse MR was null and underpowered (power 0.14–0.26). One BMD-raising profile thus protects against fracture yet raises knee-OA risk; the asymmetry rests on the forward estimates, whose overlap-induced bias is bounded at ≤2.6%.

