## 1. Introduction

Osteoarthritis (OA) and bone mineral density (BMD) are the two most prevalent skeletal traits in ageing populations, and the nature of their epidemiological association has been debated since Foss and Byers reported raised femoral-head BMD in osteoarthritic hips in 1972 [11]. Observational studies have linked higher areal BMD to increased OA prevalence and incidence, although osteoporosis and OA were long regarded as opposing conditions [12]. The direction matters clinically: if high BMD predisposes to OA, treatments that increase areal BMD may carry an OA cost — though whether that cost is mediated by bone mass or by skeletal size has not been partitioned. If OA raises BMD through altered joint loading, the interpretation differs.

Genome-wide data can separate these explanations. Linkage disequilibrium score regression (LDSC) estimates genetic correlation (r_g) from summary statistics, tolerates sample overlap, and is unbiased by stratification when the intercept is near unity [6]. Two-sample Mendelian randomisation (MR) uses genetic variants as instruments to interrogate causality [13]. Applied to OA and BMD, both have given partial answers: Hackinger et al. reported r_g=+0.18 between hip/knee OA and lumbar-spine BMD (p=0.022) [4]; Zhou et al. reported r_g=+0.13 by LDSC and +0.17 by high-definition likelihood between hip OA and femoral-neck BMD, with bidirectional MR supporting a negative causal effect of femoral-neck BMD on hip OA [5]. Our BMD→knee-OA analysis, by contrast, finds a positive effect (higher BMD raising knee-OA risk) that contrasts with Zhou's negative femoral-neck estimate; the hip-versus-knee direction reversal is discussed in the Discussion.

Three questions remain open. First, most analyses included at most two BMD sites, so site specificity is unknown, even though OA is joint-localised and BMD is regulated site by site. Second, OA has usually been represented by a single definition, leaving it unclear whether radiographic OA and self-reported OA relate to bone in the same way. Third, the OA–BMD–fracture triangle has not been analysed jointly. Because fracture is the established negative correlate of BMD, modelling OA, BMD and fracture together tests the pipeline and asks whether the OA–BMD association carries any fracture relevance.

We addressed all three in 13 skeletal phenotypes (two OA definitions, ten regional BMD sites, and any-fracture), using LDSC across all 78 phenotype pairs, two-sample MR for causal direction with an internal positive control, and reverse MR to distinguish BMD→OA from OA→BMD.

## 2. Methods

This study followed the STROBE-MR reporting guideline [Skrivankova et al., BMJ 2021]. Analyses were performed in Python 3.13 with numpy, pandas, scipy and matplotlib; the sample-overlap correction used R 4.6.1 with MRlap v0.0.3.3. All code is in the project repository (Data availability).

**Data sources.** Thirteen GWAS summary-statistic datasets were analysed: radiographic knee OA (OA_knee; GO Consortium 2025, N≈1.21M) [1], self-reported OA (OA_selfrep; IEU OpenGWAS `ukb-b-14486`, N=462,933; 38,472 cases) [3], ten regional BMD sites (Qian et al. 2025, N≈31,873 each) [2], and any-fracture (GEFOS 2018, N=264,973; 37,857 cases) [10]. Full accessions are in the Data availability statement. All source GWAS were approved by their original ethics committees; this secondary analysis required no new ethical approval.

**LDSC.** Genetic correlation (r_g) was estimated by LDSC [6] natively in Python from the Bulik-Sullivan implementation, on a common set of 690,150 HapMap3 SNPs with 1000 Genomes Phase 3 European LD scores, using delete-one-block jackknife (100 blocks) for standard errors. Palindromic SNPs were excluded; alleles were harmonised against a single reference so every cell of the r_g matrix is estimated on the same variants. Both Bonferroni (α=0.05/78) and Benjamini–Hochberg FDR were applied across 78 phenotype pairs.

**MR.** Instruments were selected at p<5×10⁻⁸ and clumped at r²<0.001 over 10 Mb against 1000 Genomes Phase 3 EUR (strict PLINK-equivalent clumping, benchmarked against the PLINK binary; 20–35 instruments retained per analysed BMD site and 116 for knee OA). Harmonisation against each outcome — exclusion of palindromic and strand-ambiguous variants and of instruments absent from the outcome GWAS — reduced the analysed sets to 16–31 instruments per forward pair and 69 per knee-OA-exposure pair; 47 of the 116 knee-OA instruments (41%) were lost at this step, an attrition we report explicitly (Supplementary Fig. S9). We computed IVW (fixed and random effects), MR-Egger (intercept test for directional pleiotropy), weighted median (Bowden’s inverse-variance weighted median of the Wald ratios) and leave-one-out, with Cochran Q and I² for heterogeneity and MR-PRESSO for pleiotropic outliers. Random-effects IVW inflated the standard error multiplicatively (SE_random = SE_fixed × √max(1, Q/df)) rather than by additive τ². BMD→fracture served as a positive control (higher BMD should protect against fracture). The OA_selfrep dataset was independently validated genome-wide against the GO Consortium 2025 knee-OA GWAS (1,980 genome-wide-significant loci, 94.4% Z-sign concordance, no global flip).

**Sample overlap.** All pairs share UK Biobank participants, partially violating the two-sample assumption. Under the Burgess approximation [14], IVW bias equals the overlap fraction × r_g and is bounded above by 2.6% for BMD→OA and 12% for BMD→fracture; it acts in the direction of r_g without changing signs or p-values. We report MR-Egger, weighted median and MR-PRESSO alongside IVW and applied an overlap-robust correction (MRlap) [15] to all ten pairs; MRlap moved every estimate further from the null (median 20%), so the primary estimates are conservative with respect to overlap. We therefore report MRlap alongside, rather than instead of, the primary IVW.

**Effect-scale reconstruction.** BMD statistics had initially been standardised against a placeholder N of 400,000; we therefore re-standardised them to per-SD units using the verified per-site N (unified N=31,873). We confirmed empirically that the betas entering the analysis are already on the true-N scale: for every instrument the reported standard error matches the closed-form expectation for a standardised effect, SE = 1/√[2·N·f·(1−f)] with f the minor-allele frequency, agreeing with N=31,873 to within 0.1% and excluding the placeholder N=400,000, which would predict standard errors 3.54-fold smaller. No post-hoc rescaling was therefore applied to any reported estimate. Because the same verified per-SD exposure units enter the forward and reverse analyses, all estimates share one N-verified SD scale, and directions, p-values and significance are invariant to it.

**Colocalisation.** Bayesian colocalisation (coloc.abf; Giambartolomei et al. 2014) tested the three a priori loci (WNT16, SOX6, GDF5) and all 31 BMD instrument loci (±500 kb; PP.H4≥0.8), with a BMD–fracture pipeline control.

**MVMR.** Multivariable MR conditioned the BMD→OA effect on adult height (Yengo et al. 2022, N=5.0M) [19] as a skeletal-size proxy, using the MVMR-IVW estimator [20] and MVMR-Egger [21], judged by the Sanderson–Smith conditional F (Fc≥10). The primary configuration requested up to n_h=100 height instruments (49–50 survived harmonisation; Yengo et al. 2022); a grid (n_h ∈ {0,100,300,1,000}) and both EUR and EUR_noUKB height sources are in the Supplementary Material.

## 3. Results

**Genetic-correlation architecture.** SNP-based heritability ranged 0.26–0.39 across the ten BMD sites and was 0.035 (knee OA), 0.022 (self-reported OA) and 0.026 (fracture); 12/13 LDSC intercepts fell between 1.01 and 1.07 (fracture 0.96), indicating little stratification. The 13×13 r_g matrix showed three blocks (Figure 1, Table 2). The ten BMD sites inter-correlated strongly (r_g 0.69–0.96, mean 0.81). Both OA definitions correlated positively with all ten BMD sites (knee OA +0.14 to +0.19; self-reported OA +0.12 to +0.25; 20/20 significant) and with each other (r_g=+0.84). Any-fracture correlated negatively with every BMD site (r_g −0.45 to −0.60; all q_FDR<0.001) and positively but non-significantly with both OA definitions (+0.15, +0.21). Of 78 pairs, 76 were significant at BH-FDR and 75 at Bonferroni; the two OA–fracture pairs were the expected null.

**Positive control: BMD protects against fracture.** Two-sample MR using 31 independent total-body BMD instruments showed genetically predicted higher BMD reduces fracture risk (IVW β=−0.337 per SD, SE 0.022, p=3.7×10⁻⁵¹; OR 1.40, 95% CI 1.34–1.46, per SD lower BMD). The estimate held under random effects (p=2.4×10⁻¹²), the MR-Egger intercept was null (p=0.558), weighted median agreed (−0.364), and leave-one-out produced no sign reversal. The femoral-neck set (25 SNPs) gave a concordant result (β=−0.351; OR 1.42, 1.34–1.51); the conservative 1-Mb set gave β=−0.366 (OR 1.44). These agree with the published GEFOS estimate (OR 1.55, 1.48–1.63) [10].

**BMD raises knee-OA risk.** Across five BMD exposures MR supported a positive effect (Table 3): total-body β=+0.023 (p=3.9×10⁻¹⁰), lumbar-spine +0.028 (p=7.7×10⁻⁹), arms +0.022 (p=1.7×10⁻⁶), legs +0.018 (p=2.3×10⁻⁵), femoral-neck +0.017 (p=6.4×10⁻⁴). Direction was consistent across all five; MR-Egger intercepts were all p>0.24; weighted medians matched the IVW sign; leave-one-out produced no sign reversals; and directions agreed with r_g (+0.12 to +0.25). Heterogeneity was high (I²=68–86%), and under random-effects IVW only two of five exposures remained significant (total-body p=0.006, lumbar-spine p=0.017); this count depends on the random-effects convention, because a DerSimonian–Laird additive-τ² implementation additionally retains the arms exposure under random effects (p=0.033). We therefore base the causal reading on the concordance of pleiotropy-robust estimators rather than on fixed-effect p-values; all five effects remained positively causal after MR-PRESSO outlier removal, with four of five strengthening. The positive control was comparably heterogeneous (I² 78% and 83% for the two BMD→fracture sets), indicating that high I² is a property of the BMD instrument sets rather than an OA-specific failure of the IVW assumptions. A multi-estimator robustness overview of all ten pairs is given in Supplementary Fig. S11.

**Reverse MR and OA→fracture are null and underpowered.** Knee OA as exposure gave non-significant effects on total-body BMD (β=+0.106, p=0.33), femoral-neck BMD (β=+0.142, p=0.19) and fracture (β=+0.092, p=0.40). These three estimates were weakly powered (achieved power 0.14–0.26; Table 4), excluding |β|≥0.30 but not moderate effects. Removing the MR-PRESSO outliers flagged for these pairs moved two of the three point estimates substantially (total-body BMD to +0.171, fracture to +0.200) without altering their non-significance, so the nulls reflect both low power and sensitivity to instrument selection.

**MR-PRESSO and robustness.** The distortion test was significant for 9/10 pairs (expected from high I²) and identified 0–3 outliers per pair; removing outliers did not reverse any direction, and strict-clumped estimates reproduced the conservative 1-Mb clumping. All significant findings survived Bonferroni correction (α=0.005).

**Effect-scale validation.** On the verified per-SD scale, the BMD→fracture OR (1.42 femoral-neck) lay 8.4% below GEFOS (1.55) on the odds-ratio scale and 20.0% below on the log-odds scale — we give both because effect estimates are compared on the log scale — with overlapping CIs; the total-body OR 1.40 was 9.6% below on the ratio scale and 23.1% below on the log-odds scale (cross-site approximation). MRlap confirmed every estimate moved further from the null (median 20%).

**Colocalisation.** At WNT16, SOX6 and GDF5 — loci nominated as shared effectors — colocalisation with knee OA was not supported (PP.H4 ≤0.005 at each; e.g. WNT16 knee-OA z=0.94, p=0.35, PP.H4=0.002; GDF5 knee-OA z=−13.7 but no BMD genome-wide signal within ±500 kb, PP.H4 0.003–0.017). Across all 31 BMD instrument loci, none reached PP.H4≥0.8 (median 0.005); six favoured distinct variants (PP.H3≥0.8). One locus fell marginally short: rs11228269 (chr11:68.29 Mb, in the LRP5 region; BMD z=−8.65, knee-OA z=−4.06, direction-concordant) gave PP.H4=0.799, i.e. 0.0006 below the pre-specified threshold, with the residual posterior mass on a BMD-only signal (PP.H1=0.156). We retain the pre-specified threshold and do not count this as colocalisation, but report it explicitly because the posterior is continuous and the 0.8 cut-off is a convention; a reader applying a 0.75 threshold would read this locus as shared. The pipeline detected colocalisation where expected: at WNT16, BMD versus fracture gave PP.H4=0.999–1.000. The knee-OA null reflects power — under the MR estimate, the expected knee-OA z at the strongest instrument is 0.85, and reaching genome-wide significance would require ~5×10⁷ participants (~42× the present sample).

**MVMR: how much of the BMD→OA effect is skeletal size?** Conditioning the BMD→OA effect on height attenuated it by 9.5–33.2% across the two tabulated univariable baselines (13.5–33.2% against the primary Table 3 baselines and 9.5–21.2% against the MVMR-set baselines; primary n_h=100/EUR; Supplementary Tables S9 and S10 and Supplementary Fig. S10), leaving 67–91% independent of the height–size pathway. The positive control confirmed a true mass effect survives conditioning: BMD→fracture moved only −1.7% to −3.0%. Because conditioning on height captured a genuine, direct OA-raising height effect (θ_H=+0.043 to +0.049, p≈10⁻¹⁶ to 10⁻¹⁹), the attenuation reflects size confounding, not dilution by a null covariate.

## 4. Discussion

**Principal findings.** Jointly analysing 13 skeletal phenotypes gave a consistent architecture: BMD sites form a strongly inter-correlated cluster, OA correlates positively with every BMD site, fracture correlates negatively with every BMD site, and the two OA definitions correlate strongly with each other. On the pleiotropy-robust evidence, genetically higher BMD raises knee-OA risk; the reverse (OA→BMD) and OA→fracture estimates were non-significant and underpowered.

**Resolving the OA–BMD paradox.** High BMD protects against fracture, confirmed here as a positive control, yet the same variants that raise BMD also raise OA risk. Subchondral bone sclerosis and osteophyte formation are hallmarks of OA and may share regulatory programs with systemic bone accrual [27–30]. That BMD protects against fracture yet raises OA risk implies a single BMD-increasing profile has opposing consequences at the two endpoints. One non-exclusive explanation — that the BMD→OA effect is partly driven by skeletal size rather than bone mass — is now quantified: areal BMD (DXA) is bone mineral content divided by projected area and is confounded by skeletal size [18,32]. MVMR conditioning on height attenuated the effect by 9.5–33.2%, while the fracture positive control moved only −1.7% to −3.0%, and height itself carried a direct OA-raising effect consistent with established epidemiology [24,25]. The height-independent share (67–91%) is therefore an upper bound on the mass-attributable component, pending a direct volumetric decomposition (test F2).

**Site and direction.** Zhou et al. [5] reported higher femoral-neck BMD protected against hip OA, whereas our ten-site analysis shows higher BMD raises knee-OA risk — a hip-versus-knee contrast explicable under joint-specific pathway dominance (hip OA is mechanically/morphologically driven, knee OA more by systemic bone-formation pathways). This cautions against generalising any single BMD→OA estimate across joints. Hartley et al. [22] reported a positive BMD effect on both hip (OR 1.28, 1.05–1.57) and knee OA (OR 1.40, 1.20–1.63); Funck-Brentano et al. [36] likewise reported positive effects at both joints (knee OR 1.20, 1.05–1.37; hip OR 1.23, 1.12–1.36); a quantitative comparison on the native scales is given in Supplementary Fig. S12. These run against the directional form of our prediction P6 (motivated by Zhou's protective femoral-neck finding). We adjudicate rather than cite selectively. First, the hip evidence disagrees with itself (protective [5], harmful [22], harmful [36]). Second, and decisively for the falsification rule F1, the criterion is conjunctive: the hip effect must be positive AND of knee-like magnitude. Within Hartley et al. — the only same-pipeline comparison — the hip and knee intervals overlap almost completely (1.05–1.57 vs 1.20–1.63) and on the log-odds scale the hip point estimate lies 27% below the knee's (0.247 vs 0.336); Hartley establishes positivity, not equality. We therefore restate P6 as a magnitude claim rather than a directional one, and pre-register the decision rule (a hip effect that is positive and at least as large as the knee's would fail P6).

**Biological context and predictions.** OA is a disorder of the osteochondral unit [27–30], in which subchondral bone remodelling feeds cartilage degradation. A systemic, polygenic bone-forming set-point readable as areal BMD would raise fracture protection at the whole-bone endpoint and raise OA risk at the joint endpoint through increased subchondral bone formation. The set-point is operationalised as areal BMD, a composite of volumetric density and size that we partition only partially here (67–91% height-independent); it is a latent tendency indexed by areal BMD, not a directly measured quantity. The reading generates six predictions (P1–P6) and four falsifying tests (F1–F4; Supplementary Material): P1–P4 are supported in direction and F1 is pre-registered; the construct's mass-versus-size composition is bounded here but resolved only by test F2.

**OA→fracture: no supported pathway.** Neither r_g (+0.15, +0.21) nor MR (β=+0.092, p=0.40) supported a causal OA→fracture link; this is the first direct MR test of OA→any-fracture. The estimate excludes large but not moderate effects (power 0.14).

**Strengths and limitations.** Strengths: ten BMD sites with two OA definitions; an internal BMD–fracture positive control calibrated against the published GEFOS estimate (OR 1.40–1.42 vs 1.55); dual FDR/Bonferroni correction; a common SNP set throughout; and directional concordance across IVW, MR-Egger, weighted median and MR-PRESSO. Limitations: areal BMD conflates size with mass, and although height-conditioning bounds the size-attributable share at 9.5–33.2%, a direct volumetric decomposition is not yet available; heterogeneity is high across all pairs including the positive control, so the causal claim rests on estimator concordance rather than on fixed-effect p-values; the reverse and OA→fracture estimates are null but weakly powered (0.14–0.26), excluding only |β|≥0.30; and residual sample overlap, although bounded at ≤2.6% for BMD→OA and acting in the observed direction, cannot be removed with summary data alone.

**Conclusion.** Genetically higher BMD protects against fracture and, on the pleiotropy-robust evidence, raises knee-OA risk. The effect pertains to the clinically measured areal-BMD phenotype, 67–91% of it independent of skeletal size; reverse causation and an OA→fracture pathway were not supported, although the reverse estimates exclude only |β|≥0.30. Trials of treatments that raise areal BMD should record incident OA at the joint of interest, not fracture alone.

